{title: Wayfaring Stranger}
{artist: Traditional American}
{key: Am}
{capo: 2}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 68}
{tag: Slow}
{meta: language en}

{comment: With the capo on the second fret this sits nicely under most voices. Take it slowly.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
I'm just a [Am]poor wayfaring [Dm]stranger
A-[Am]travelling through this world of [E7]woe
But there's no [Am]sickness, toil or [Dm]danger
In that bright [Am]land to [E7]which I [Am]go
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
I'm going [Am]there to see my [Dm]father
I'm going [Am]there no more to [E7]roam
I'm only [Am]going over [Dm]Jordan
I'm only [Am]going [E7]over [Am]home
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
I know dark [Am]clouds will gather [Dm]round me
I know my [Am]way is rough and [E7]steep
But golden [Am]fields lie out be[Dm]fore me
Where God's re[Am]deemed [E7]their vigils [Am]keep
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_bridge: Bridge}
I'll soon be [Dm]free from every [Am]trial
This form shall [Dm]rest beneath the [E7]sod
{end_of_bridge}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
I'll drop the [Am]cross of self de[Dm]nial
And enter [Am]in that [E7]home with [Am]God
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
